    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        Natural Language <span class="search-hit mathjax">Tools</span>: A Natural Language Approach to <span class="search-hit mathjax">Tool</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">Calling</span> In Large Language <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agents</span>
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Johnson%2C+R+T">Reid T. Johnson</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Pain%2C+M+D">Michelle D. Pain</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=West%2C+J+D">Jordan D. West</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="search-hit">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2510.14453v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        We present Natural Language <span class="search-hit mathjax">Tools</span> (NLT), a framework that replaces programmatic JSON&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2510.14453v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2510.14453v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2510.14453v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        We present Natural Language <span class="search-hit mathjax">Tools</span> (NLT), a framework that replaces programmatic JSON <span class="search-hit mathjax">tool</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">calling</span> in large language models (<span class="search-hit mathjax">LLMs</span>) with natural language outputs. By decoupling <span class="search-hit mathjax">tool</span> selection from response <span class="search-hit mathjax">generation</span>, NLT eliminates task interference and format constraints that degrade <span class="search-hit mathjax">tool</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">call</span> performance. When evaluated across 10 models and 6,400 trials spanning customer service and mental health domains, NLT improves <span class="search-hit mathjax">tool</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">calling</span> accuracy by 18.4 percentage points while reducing output variance by 70%. Open-weight models see the largest gains, surpassing flagship closed-weight alternatives, with implications for model training in both reinforcement learning and supervised fine-tuning stages. These improvements persist under prompt perturbations and extend <span class="search-hit mathjax">tool</span>-<span class="search-hit mathjax">calling</span> capabilities to models lacking native support.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2510.14453v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2510.14453v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 16 October, 2025; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> October 2025.
      
    </p>
    
